---
import StaticPage from '../layouts/StaticPage.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config/site';
---

<StaticPage path="/editorial-policy/" h1="Editorial policy" updated="2026-11-23">
  <p class="mt-6">
    This page explains how every calculator on Opus Calculator is written, tested, reviewed and corrected, so you can judge how far to trust a result.
  </p>

  <h2>How a calculator is written</h2>
  <p>
    Each calculator starts with a plain description of what it works out, the exact formula, and a worked example done by hand. The page text opens with the
    answer for a realistic set of numbers, then explains the method, the common mistakes and what the result does not include (for example, that a mortgage
    payment here excludes property tax and insurance). Text is written for each calculator; pages are not produced by swapping a word into a template.
  </p>

  <h2>How a calculator is tested</h2>
  <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li>
      Every calculator has automatic tests with known answers calculated independently of the code, including edge cases such as zero, empty and very large
      values.
    </li>
    <li>All money, percentage and ratio arithmetic uses exact decimal numbers. Results are rounded to the cent the way a lender or shop would.</li>
    <li>
      The mortgage and loan calculators are compared with a separate month-by-month repayment schedule and must match to the cent. Where a calculator follows a
      published standard or a regulated rule, the source is recorded in its tests.
    </li>
    <li>
      The default result is part of the page HTML, and a test checks that the number quoted in the page text is the number the calculator really produces.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The tier system and the quality gate</h2>
  <p>
    Calculators are grouped into three tiers. Tier 1 are hand-written pages for the most common needs. Tier 2 are common variations, such as a mortgage for a
    particular country and term. Tier 3 are long-tail variations. Before any calculator can be published it must pass an automatic quality gate: enough original
    explanatory text, a worked example, at least three frequently asked questions with real answers, results that depend only on the numbers you type, and text
    that is clearly different from every other page. A page that fails is not published and cannot be linked from anywhere.
  </p>
  <p>
    Pages are also released in stages ("cohorts"). A later stage is published only after the earlier stage has been indexed by search engines and is being read
    by people. This keeps the site from adding pages faster than it can keep them accurate.
  </p>

  <h2>Review of health, finance, construction, food and science calculators</h2>
  <p>
    Calculators in these categories show a disclaimer under the result and a "last reviewed" date. Where a qualified reviewer has signed off a category, the
    page names the reviewer and their credentials. The site does not invent credentials, and a reviewer is named only with their agreement.
  </p>

  <h2 id="unaudited">Unaudited calculators</h2>
  <p>
    At present no category has an external professional reviewer. Until one is named on a page, treat its result as an estimate that has been tested for
    arithmetic correctness but not reviewed by a licensed professional. For these pages the "last reviewed" date is the date the calculator was last updated by
    the site's owner, and the page does not name a reviewer. The disclaimer under each result applies in full: check anything that affects your health, your
    money or a building with a qualified professional.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections and reporting an error</h2>
  <p>
    If a result looks wrong, write to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email.general}`}>{SITE.email.general}</a> with the calculator, the numbers you typed, the result
    you got and the one you expected. We confirm the problem, fix the calculator and its tests, and update the "last updated" date on the page. If a correction
    changes a result that people may have relied on, the page says what changed.
  </p>

  <h2>Independence</h2>
  <p>
    The site is paid for by one advertisement per page. Advertisers have no say in how a calculator works, which calculators exist, or what a page says. Read
    <a href="/how-we-make-money/">how we make money</a>.
  </p>
</StaticPage>
